Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.1", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The first new program produces the system of differential equations for the \
asymptotic curves.  The relevant coefficients ",
 StyleBox["et,ft,gt",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " are proportional to the second fundamental form coefficients ",
 StyleBox["eee, fff, ggg.  ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "In theory, the latter could have been generated using",
 StyleBox[" shape ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "and used instead, but what follows has the advantage of being \
self-contained."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
asymeq1[x_][u_,v_]:=
Module[{U,V,a},
   xu= D[x[U,V], U];
   xv= D[x[U,V], V];
   xuu= D[x[U,V],U,U];
   xuv= D[x[U,V],U,V];
   xvv= D[x[U,V],V,V];
   et:= Det[{xuu,xu,xv}];
   ft:= Det[{xuv,xu,xv}];
   gt:= Det[{xvv,xu,xv}]; 
   so:= Solve[et a^2 + 2ft a + gt == 0, a];
   Table[u'[v]\[Equal]a/.so[[i]], {i,2}]/.{U->u[v],V->v}
]\
\>", "Definition",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["The next output gives the system for a monkey saddle.", "Text"],

Cell["asymeq1[monkeysaddle][u,v]", "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here is the program from Notebook 7 to compute the torsion of a space \
curve, used in the Beltrami-Enneper Theorem, and applied in ",
 StyleBox["N18.2.",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
\[Tau][\[Alpha]_][t_]:= 
Module[{tt,a,B},
   a= Det[{\[Alpha]'[tt], \[Alpha]''[tt], \[Alpha]'''[tt]}];
   B= Cross[\[Alpha]'[tt], \[Alpha]''[tt]];
   a/(B.B) /.tt->t
]\
\>", "Miniprogram"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.2", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The circular helix was introduced in ",
 StyleBox["N7.3.",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "  Here is an elliptical version:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["helix[a_,b_,c_][t_]:={a Cos[t], b Sin[t], c t}", "Definition",
 InitializationCell->True,
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["It is a coordinate curve on the elliptical helicoid:", "Text"],

Cell["helicoid[a,b,c][t,1]", "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
The inner and outer edges of the elliptical helicoid shown in the next figure \
are also helices.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
he:= helicoid[1,2,1]
S @ ParametricPlot3D[he[u,v], {u,-\[Pi],5\[Pi]}, {v,1/3,3},
   PlotPoints->{80,10}]\
\>", "NumberedFigure",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The coefficients of the elliptical helicoid's second fundamental form are \
output below.  Only the middle one, ",
 StyleBox["fff ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "(standing for f), is nonzero."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
shape[helicoid[a,b,c]][u,v];
eee
fff
ggg\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Next, we compute the Gaussian curvature of the helicoid and the torsion of \
an associated helix.  The output of the last line is ",
 StyleBox["0,",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " and this verifies an instance of the Beltrami-Enneper Theorem."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
oid:= gcurv[helicoid[a,b,c]]
oid[u,v]
ix:= \[Tau][helix[a v, b v, c]]
oid[u,v]+ix[u]^2//Simplify\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
The next few cells investigate asymptotic curves on the exponentially-twisted \
helicoid.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["exptwist[a_,c_][u_,v_]:={a v Cos[u], a v Sin[u], a Exp[c u]}", \
"Definition",
 InitializationCell->True,
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
ParametricPlot3D[exptwist[1, 0.3`][u, v], {u, -\[Pi], 2 \[Pi]}, {v, -1, 1}]\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
x:= exptwist[a,c]
eqs:= asymeq1[x][u,v]
DSolve[eqs[[1]],u[v],v]/.C[1]->p
DSolve[eqs[[2]],u[v],v]/.C[1]->q\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The two expressons for ",
 StyleBox["u[v",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 StyleBox["]",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " that appear in the output enable us to define an asymptotic patch, and \
compare it with the original parametrization."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
exptwistasym[a_,c_][p_,q_]:=
   a{Exp[c(p-q)/2]Cos[p], Exp[c(p-q)/2]Sin[p], Exp[c p]}\
\>", "Definition",
 InitializationCell->True,
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{x,y,z},
   x= exptwist[1,.3];
   y= exptwistasym[1,.3];
   z[2]= ParametricPlot3D[x[u,v], {u,-\[Pi],2\[Pi]}, {v,-1,1},
      PlotPoints->{24,12}];
   z[1]= ParametricPlot3D[y[p,q], {p,-\[Pi],2\[Pi]}, {q,-\[Pi],2\[Pi]},
      PlotPoints->{36,12}, PlotRange->All];
   S @ GraphicsArray[Array[z,2], GraphicsSpacing->-.3]
]\
\>", "NumberedFigure",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
Here are some more standard computations of second fundamental forms:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
hp:= paraboloid[1,-1]
shape[hp][u,v]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
shape[monkeysaddle][u,v];
eee
fff
ggg\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Instructions to plot the funnel surface are given next, though a further \
examination is relegated to ",
 StyleBox["N18.2.1,",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " in which we explain how Figure 18.3 is plotted using the program ",
 StyleBox["selectgraphics3d.",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["funnel[a_,b_,c_][u_,v_]:={a v Cos[u], b v Sin[u], c Log[v]}", \
"Definition",
 InitializationCell->True,
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
fu:= funnel[1,1,1]
ParametricPlot3D[fu[u,v], {u,0,3\[Pi]/2}, {v,.1,1}];\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.2.1", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
shape[funnel[a,b,c]][u,v];
eee
fff
ggg\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
ParametricPlot3D[funnel[1, 2, 3][u, v], {u, 0, 2 \[Pi]}, {v, 0.01`, 3}]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
funnelasym[c_][p_,q_]:=
   {c Exp[p-q]Cos[p+q], -c Exp[p-q]Sin[p+q], c(p-q)}\
\>", "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
shape[funnelasym[a]][p,q];
eee
fff
ggg\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
selectgraphics3d[graphics3dobj_,bound_,opts___]:=
Show[Graphics3D[Select[graphics3dobj, (
Abs[#[[1,1,1]]]<bound && Abs[#[[1,1,2]]]<bound && Abs[#[[1,1,3]]]<bound && \
Abs[#[[1,2,1]]]<bound && Abs[#[[1,2,2]]]<bound && Abs[#[[1,2,3]]]<bound && \
Abs[#[[1,3,1]]]<bound && Abs[#[[1,3,2]]]<bound && Abs[#[[1,3,3]]]<bound && \
Abs[#[[1,4,1]]]<bound && Abs[#[[1,4,2]]]<bound && \
Abs[#[[1,4,2]]]<bound)&]],opts]\
\>", "Definition",
 InitializationCell->True,
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
fp:= ParametricPlot3D[funnelasym[1][u,v], {u,0,\[Pi]}, {v,0,\[Pi]}];
S @ selectgraphics3d[fp[[1]],1.8,
PlotRange->All,ViewPoint\[Rule]{1/2, -3, 1}, Boxed->False];\
\>", "NumberedFigure",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Integrate", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"c", "/", 
     RowBox[{"Sqrt", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"v", "^", "2"}], 
       RowBox[{"(", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"v", "^", "2"}], "-", 
         RowBox[{"c", "^", "2"}]}], ")"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", "v"}], "]"}], "//",
   "PowerExpand"}]], "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.3", "Section"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The following program solves numerically the equations for geodesics on a \
surface",
 StyleBox[" x[u,v]",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " emanating from a given point ",
 StyleBox["x[u0,v0]",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 ", with (arc length) parameter ranging from ",
 StyleBox["0",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " to ",
 StyleBox["b",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 ".  A geodesic is found for each of ",
 StyleBox["m",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " directions that divide the circle (in the tangent space).  The symbol ",
 StyleBox["e",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " stands for the first order part of the equations, and",
 StyleBox[" eqic",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " represents the geodesic equations themselves together with their initial \
conditions. "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
solvegeo[x_,{u0_,v0_},b_,m_]:= 
Module[{su,e,so},
   christoff2[x][u,v];
   su= {u->u[s], v->v[s], p->u'[s], q->v'[s]};
   e[j_]:= e[j]= G[j,1,1]p^2 + 2G[j,1,2]p q + G[j,2,2]q^2 /.su;
   eqic:= {u''[s]+e[1]==0, v''[s]+e[2]==0,
      u[0]==u0, v[0]==v0, u'[0]==Cos[\[Theta]], v'[0]==Sin[\[Theta]]};
   so:= NDSolve[eqic, {u,v}, {s,0,b}];
   Flatten[Table[so, {\[Theta], 2\[Pi]/m, 2\[Pi], 2\[Pi]/m}], 1]
] \
\>", "Miniprogram"],

Cell["\<\
The next figure illustrates a hyperbolic paraboloid, with saddle point (at \
the origin) marked by a red dot.  First we solve for the geodesics.  These \
emanate from a different point P, and \"geodesic circles\" join points of a \
given fixed arc length from P.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
hp:= paraboloid[1,-1]
b:= 2
m:= 50
sg:= solvegeo[hp, {1,-1}/2, b, m]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Change ",
 StyleBox["Show",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " to ",
 StyleBox["S",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " to view the next plot:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
z0:= Graphics3D[{Hue[0], PointSize[.05], Point[{0,0,0}]}]
z1:= Graphics3D[Table[Line[hp@@{u[t],v[t]}/.sg], {t,0,b,b/8}]]
z2:= ParametricPlot3D[Evaluate[hp@@{u[t],v[t]}/.sg], {t,0,b}]
Show[z0, z1, z2, AspectRatio->Automatic, Boxed->False]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.3.1", "Subsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here is the same thing for the monkeysaddle.  Observe that the geodesics \
are \"clumped\" into six directions, three of which point down the \
\"valleys\".  The command ",
 StyleBox["complete",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " is defined in ",
 StyleBox["N18.6.",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{x,b,m,n,ta,z,xx,\[Alpha],\[Alpha]\[Alpha]},
   x= monkeysaddle;
   b= 4;
   m= 120;
   n= 8;
   \[Alpha][k_]= {Cos[(4k-3)\[Pi]/6], Sin[(4k-3)\[Pi]/6], 0}# &;
   z[k_]:= ParametricPlot3D[highlight[\[Alpha][k], .6, .01][t], {t,-b,b}];
   sg= solvegeo[x,{0,0}, b, m];
   c[t_]= complete[x@@{u[t],v[t]}/.sg, Hue[.6]];
   z[4]= Graphics3D[Table[c[t], {t,0,b,b/n}]];
   xx[t_]:= x@@{u[t],v[t]}/.sg;
   z[5]= ParametricPlot3D[xx[t]//Evaluate, {t,0,b}];
   S[Array[z,5], ViewPoint->{1.1,2,2.7}, PlotRange->{-.05,4},
      Boxed->False, ImageSize->450]
];\
\>", "NumberedFigure"]
}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.4", "Section"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We first apply the geodesic plotting program to the sphere.  It produces a \
total of 10 functions of the form ",
 StyleBox["t->{u[t],v[t]}",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 ", with ",
 StyleBox["t",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " running between ",
 StyleBox["0 ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "and ",
 StyleBox["4",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 ", defined numerically; details are suppressed from the output."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
x:= sphere[1]
solvegeo[x, {0,0}, 4, 10]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The next command constructs line segments joining a given sequence of \
points, including one to join the last to the first point (hence the name ",
 StyleBox["complete",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 ").  The optional second argument will allow us to add colors, etc."
}], "Text"],

Cell["complete[seq_,opt___]:= {opt, Line[Append[seq, First[seq]]]}", \
"Definition"],

Cell["\<\
The first picture shows the geodesics, as represented in the rectangular \
chart of the sphere.  They would meet at the south pole with parameter value \
\[Pi], so stopping at 3 leaves a small gap.  The situation is of course \
better when these are plotted in space:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{x,z},
   x= sphere[1]; 
   sg= solvegeo[x, {0,0}, 3, 30];
   z[2]= ParametricPlot[Evaluate[{u[t],v[t]}/.sg], {t,0,3},
      PlotRange->{-2.5,2}, PlotStyle->Hue[0]];
   z[1]= ParametricPlot3D[Evaluate[x[u[t],v[t]]/.sg], {t,0,3}];
   S @ GraphicsArray[Array[z,2], GraphicsSpacing->0]
]\
\>", "NumberedFigure"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The next picture shows the geodesic circles, though once these are plotted \
beyond ",
 StyleBox["\[Pi]/2 ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "(the equator), they are disconnected in the chart's domain, resulting in \
spurious lines that could in theory be eliminated using a trick like the one \
explained in ",
 StyleBox["N3.2.",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Graphics", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"complete", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"u", "[", "t", "]"}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"v", "[", "t", "]"}]}], "}"}], "/.", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
        "sg"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"Hue", "[", "0", "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"t", ",", "0", ",", "3", ",", 
       FractionBox["1", "2"]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"AspectRatio", "\[Rule]", "Automatic"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Again, the situation is clearer when the chart ",
 StyleBox["x ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "is applied to map the curves onto the surface in space.  In the following \
code, one can try to see what happens if ",
 StyleBox["complete",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " is replaced by ",
 StyleBox["Line:",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Graphics3D", "[", 
  RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"complete", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"x", "@@", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"u", "[", "t", "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"v", "[", "t", "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "/.", "\[InvisibleSpace]", 
      "sg"}], "]"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"t", ",", "0", ",", "3", ",", 
      FractionBox["1", "5"]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell["We next pass to the torus:", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
to := torus[3, 1, 1] z0 := (Graphics3D z1)[{PointSize[0.05`], Point[to @@ {\
\[Pi], \[Pi]}]}] := ParametricPlot3D[to[u, v], {u, -\[Pi], \[Pi]}, {v, \
-\[Pi], \[Pi]}, ColorFunction -> (White &)] Show[z1, z0];\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{x,P,z},
   b= 2\[Pi];
   x= torus[3,1,1];
   P= {\[Pi],\[Pi]}; 
   sg= solvegeo[x, P, b, 30];
   z[2]= ParametricPlot[Evaluate[{u[t],v[t]}/.sg], {t,0,b},
      PlotRange->{-5,10}, PlotStyle->Hue[0]];
   z[1]= ParametricPlot3D[Evaluate[x[u[t],v[t]]/.sg], {t,0,b}];
   S @ GraphicsArray[Array[z,2], GraphicsSpacing->0]
]\
\>", "NumberedFigure"],

Cell["\<\
Show[Graphics[Table[complete[{u[t],v[t]}/.sg, Hue[0]],
    {t,0,b,1/2}]], AspectRatio->Automatic]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To draw the points of a given arc length from the point P with \
coordinates",
 StyleBox[" ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 StyleBox["{\[Pi],\[Pi]},",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " we need to increase the number",
 StyleBox[" m",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " of geodesics:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
b:= 2.5
m:= 200
sg:=solvegeo[to,{\[Pi],\[Pi]},b,m]
ta:= Table[complete[to@@{u[t],v[t]}/.sg, Hue[.7]], {t,0,b,b/5}]
Show[ta//Graphics3D, AspectRatio->Automatic, Boxed->False]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The next figure duplicates the biggest closed curve, which is a geodesic \
circle with center P (the black dot).  If the \"radius\"",
 StyleBox[" b ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "were greater or equal to \[Pi], the geodesic disk it bounds would no longer \
be nonsingular (homeomorphic to an ordinary disk) as its boundary would have \
self-intersections.  The geodesic disk is ",
 StyleBox["not",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " the thin strip of torus represented by the \"tongue\" in Figure 18.7 but \
the curved \"tube\" lying to one side of it.  The definition of ",
 StyleBox["z2",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " is an explicit variant of ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "'s ",
 StyleBox["WireFrame ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "construction, and is recorded more formally in ",
 StyleBox["N18.4.1.",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{ta},
   ta= complete[to@@{u[b],v[b]}/.sg, Hue[.7], Thickness[.01]];
   z0= {PointSize[.03], Point[to[\[Pi],\[Pi]]]}//Graphics3D;
   z1:= ParametricPlot3D[to[u,v], {u,-3,3}, {v,-\[Pi],\[Pi]},
      PlotPoints->20];
   z2:= z1/.(Polygon[pts_]:>complete[pts, Hue[.1]]);
   S[z0, z2, ta//Graphics3D, Boxed->False, Shading->False]
]\
\>", "NumberedFigure"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.4.1", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
wireframe[x_,{u_,u0_,u1_},{v_,v0_,v1_}, opts_]:=
Module[{z,zz},
   z=  ParametricPlot3D[x[u,v], {u,u0,u1}, {v,v0,v1}, opts];
   zz=z/.(Polygon[pts_]:>complete[pts]);
   Show[zz]
]\
\>", "Miniprogram"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{x,z},
   x[u_,v_]= {u, Sin[u], v};
   z[1]= wireframe[x, {u,0,3\[Pi]}, {v,0,2\[Pi]},
      PlotPoints->{15,10}];
   \[Alpha][u_]:= {u, Sin[u], 2u/3};
   \[Alpha]\[Alpha]= highlight[\[Alpha]];   
   z[2]= ParametricPlot3D[\[Alpha]\[Alpha][u], {u,0,3\[Pi]}];
   S @ Array[z,2]
];\
\>", "NumberedFigure",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.5", "Section"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Most of the examples in this notebook section are based on Corollary 18.29 \
that characterizes geodesics in a Clairaut ",
 StyleBox["v",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " patch.  The following program solves the resulting geodesic equation."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
clairautv[pm_,c_,u0_][x_][u_,t_]:=
Module[{Q,R,tt},
   metric[x][u,tt];
   Q= Sqrt[c^2 gg/(ee(ee-c^2))];
   R= u0+Integrate[pm Q, tt]/.tt->t;
   PowerExpand[R]
]\
\>", "Miniprogram",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Although the function ",
 StyleBox["clairautv",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " has ",
 StyleBox["u",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " as an argument, its value will not depend on ",
 StyleBox["u",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " if",
 StyleBox[" x",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " is a Clairaut ",
 StyleBox["v ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "patch.  The construction is first applied in the plane by means of"
}], "Text"],

Cell["polarplane[u_,v_]:={v Cos[u], v Sin[u], 0}", "Definition",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["The complicated looking solution is indeed a straight line.", "Text"],

Cell["f[t_]= clairautv[1,2,1][polarplane][1,t]", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
\[Gamma][t_]:= {t Cos[f[t]], t Sin[f[t]]}
ParametricPlot[\[Gamma][t], {t,5,9}, Axes->True];\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The circular cone, studied in ",
 StyleBox["N12.2, ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "is another surface with zero Gaussian curvature."
}], "Text"],

Cell["clairautv[pm,c,u0][circularcone[a,b]][u,t]", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The next program parametrizes a geodesic on a Clairaut",
 StyleBox[" v",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " patch in space, and is i",
 StyleBox["llustrated with some simple examples.",
  FontSlant->"Plain"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
clairautgeodesic[x_,c_][t_]:= 
Module[{f},
   f[v_]= clairautv[1,c,0][x][u,v];
   x[Re[f[t]], t]
]\
\>", "Miniprogram",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
\[Gamma]:= clairautgeodesic[sphere[1], .5]
ParametricPlot3D[\[Gamma][t], {t,0,1}, Axes->True]\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
\[Gamma]:= clairautgeodesic[paraboloid[1,1], 0]
ParametricPlot3D[\[Gamma][t], {t,-1,1}, Axes->True]\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
Here we compute the equations of geodesics on a pseudosphere:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Clear[a,c,u0,pm]
clairautv[pm,c,u0][pseudosphere[a]][u,t]//FullSimplify
clairautgeodesic[pseudosphere[a],u0][t]//FullSimplify\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The simplified equation for ",
 StyleBox["u ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "output first from the previous cell is recorded below; it will be inserted \
into a plotting program in ",
 StyleBox["N18.6.",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
psclairautv[a_,pm_,c_,u0_][t_]=
   u0 + pm Sqrt[a^2-2c^2-a^2 Cos[2t]]Csc[t]/(Sqrt[2] c)\
\>", "Definition",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
Further examples of Clairaut patches are contained in the subsection that \
follows in the notebook's electronic version.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.5.1", "Subsection",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell[" Here, there is dependence on u", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
paraboloidpolar[n_,a_,b_][r_,\[Theta]_]:= {a r Cos[\[Theta]], a r \
Cos[\[Theta]], b r^n}\
\>", "Definition",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
metric[paraboloidpolar[1,1,2]][r,\[Theta]]\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
Since this is not a Clairaut patch, there will be dependence on u below.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[OutputFormData["\<\
{{5 + Cos[2*\[Theta]], -2*r*Cos[\[Theta]]*Sin[\[Theta]]}, \
{-2*r*Cos[\[Theta]]*Sin[\[Theta]], 2*r^2*Sin[\[Theta]]^2}}\
\>", "\<\
                                                             2       2
{{5 + Cos[2 \[Theta]], -2 r Cos[\[Theta]] Sin[\[Theta]]}, {-2 r Cos[\[Theta]] \
Sin[\[Theta]], 2 r  Sin[\[Theta]] }}\
\>"], "Output"],

Cell["clairautv[pm,c,u0][paraboloidpolar[n,a,b]][u,t]", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["clairautv[pm,c,u0][funnel[1,1,a]][u,t]", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["clairautv[pm,c,u0][torus[a,b,b]][u,t]", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
yyy[a_,c_,e_][t_]:=
{a Cos[a e ArcTanh[(Sqrt[2] a Sin[t])/
   Sqrt[a^2-2 c^2-a^2 Cos[2 t]]]] Sin[t],
a Sin[t] Sin[a e ArcTanh[(Sqrt[2] a Sin[t])/
Sqrt[a^2-2 c^2-a^2 Cos[2 t]]]], a (Cos[t]+Log[Tan[t/2]])}\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["\<\
clairaut[pm_,c_,u0_][x_][u_,t_]:=
   PowerExpand[u0+Integrate[pm Sqrt[c^2 gg[x][u,tt]/
   (ee[x][u,tt] (ee[x][u,tt]-c^2))],tt]]/.tt\[Rule]t\
\>", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"],

Cell["FullSimplify[clairaut[pm,c,u0][pseudosphere[a]][u,t]]", "Input",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.6", "Section"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We repeat what we did above for the torus.  First, we slightly alter the \
definition of the pseudosphere to avoid difficulties in applying the \
numerical method, and multiply by ",
 StyleBox["0.99",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " to make the dot and geodesics visible."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"psm", "[", 
   RowBox[{"u_", ",", "v_"}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "u", "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "v", "]"}]}], ",", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "u", "]"}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "v", "]"}]}], ",", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "v", "]"}], "+", 
       RowBox[{"Log", "[", 
        RowBox[{"0.001`", "\[InvisibleSpace]", "+", 
         RowBox[{"Tan", "[", 
          FractionBox["v", "2"], "]"}]}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}], " ", "P"}], ":=", 
   
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"5.3`", ",", "2.5`"}], "}"}], " ", "z0"}], ":=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{"Graphics3D", " ", "z1"}], ")"}], "[", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"PointSize", "[", "0.05`", "]"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"Point", "[", 
         RowBox[{"psm", "@@", "P"}], "]"}]}], "}"}], "]"}], ":=", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"ParametricPlot3D", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"0.99`", " ", 
         RowBox[{"psm", "[", 
          RowBox[{"u", ",", "v"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"u", ",", "0", ",", 
          RowBox[{"2", " ", "\[Pi]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"v", ",", 
          FractionBox["\[Pi]", "2"], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"ColorFunction", "\[Rule]", 
         RowBox[{"(", 
          RowBox[{"White", "&"}], ")"}]}]}], "]"}], " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"z1", ",", "z0"}], "}"}]}]}]}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The next cell customizes the program ",
 StyleBox["solvegeo ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "above to cope with the pseudosphere.  There are two main modifications: \
first we require geodesics spanning only the first quadrant, and we also wish \
each to have a different color:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
sg:= Module[{su,e,so},
   christoff2[psm][u,v];
   su= {u->u[s], v->v[s], p->u'[s], q->v'[s]};
   e[j_]:= e[j]= G[j,1,1]p^2 + 2G[j,1,2]p q + G[j,2,2]q^2 /.su;
   eqic:= {u''[s]+e[1]==0, v''[s]+e[2]==0,
      u[0]==P[[1]], v[0]==P[[2]], 
      u'[0]==Cos[\[Theta]], v'[0]==Sin[\[Theta]]};
   so:= NDSolve[eqic, {u,v}, {s,0,5}];
   Flatten[Table[so, {\[Theta],0,\[Pi]/2,\[Pi]/8}], 1]
]             \
\>", "Miniprogram"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{\[Alpha],\[Alpha]\[Alpha],z},
   z0:= {PointSize[.03], Point[psm@@P]}//Graphics3D;
   z1:= ParametricPlot3D[.99psm[u,v], {u,0,2\[Pi]}, {v,\[Pi]/2,\[Pi]}];
   z2:= z1/.(Polygon[pts_]:>complete[pts, GrayLevel[.8]]);
   \[Alpha][i_][t_]:= psm@@{u[t],v[t]}/.sg[[i+1]];
   \[Alpha]\[Alpha][i_]:= highlight[\[Alpha][i], i/5,1/100];
   z[i_]:= ParametricPlot3D[\[Alpha]\[Alpha][i-1][t]//Evaluate, {t,0,.8i+1}];
   S[z2, z0, Array[z,5], ImageSize->400]
]\
\>", "NumberedFigure"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Changing the second coordinate of P from 2.5 to 2.7 as a dramatic effect.  \
Using the explicit expression found in ",
 StyleBox["N18.5 ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "for a geodesic on the pseudosphere, we plot ",
 StyleBox["u",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " modulo ",
 StyleBox["2\[Pi]",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 " as a function of",
 StyleBox[" t ",
  FontFamily->"Verdana"],
 "to see how a geodesic winds its way up the neck."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
psg[c_,t_]:= psclairautv[1,1,c,0][t]//N
f[c_,t_]:= 2\[Pi](psg[c,t]/(2\[Pi]) - Floor[psg[c,t]/(2\[Pi])])
Plot[f[.02,t], {t, 3.10, 3.12}]\
\>", "Definition",
 FontFamily->"Verdana"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["N18.7", "Section"],

Cell["\<\
The first command can (with appropriate sign choices) be used for both types \
of paraboloid:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{h,x,z},
   h[u_,v_]= (u^2+v^2)^(-1/2); 
   x[u_,v_]= {u, v, h[u,v]};
   y[u_,v_]= gcurv[x][u,v]/2;
   xx[u_,v_]= Append[x[u,v], FaceForm[Hue[.2+1.5y[u,v]]]];
   S @ ParametricPlot3D[xx[u,v], {u,-2,2}, {v,-2,2},
      PlotPoints->40, Lighting->False, ImageSize->350,
      PlotRange->{0,3}]
]\
\>", "NumberedFigure"]
}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]]
},
